  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 301 AM MDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nAnother round of showers and thunderstorms expected today as above \nnormal moisture remains in place for one more day. Main concern \ncontinues to be localized heavy rainfall producing flash \nflooding, particularly for burn scar areas in the mountains, and \nportions of the plains. Let's break it down below: \n\nGuidance continues to favor above normal precipitable water values \n>1\", which is above the 90th percentile for the Denver area. \nForecast soundings show tall, thin CAPE (a typical profile for \nmaximizing precipitation efficiency) with a relatively moist \nvertical profile and a warm cloud layer depth of 4-5,000 ft. These \ningredients coupled with expected slow storm motion, will promote \nheavy rainfall and localized flash flooding. A flash flood watch was \nconsidered for portions of the plains, including the southern I-25\ncorridor and adjacent areas east. However, recent REFS 3hr LPMM \nQPF is nowhere near as robust as it was for Tuesday's convection \n(suggesting 0.75\"-1.25\" along the urban corridor with the \nstrongest storms for today). The most likely reason for this is \ndue to the lack of decent instability compared to yesterday, with \nmost CAMs indicating >1000 J/Kg of MLCAPE for the urban corridor \n(better instability farther east). For this reason, have held off \non a watch for now, but if models begin changing and aligning more\nwith the NAM (the more bullish model for today's convection), the\nnext forecast package may need one. \n\nTiming: Widespread convection is expected to begin by the late \nmorning/early afternoon across the high terrain, before spilling \nonto the plains and trekking east throughout the \nafternoon/evening. Best chance for the Urban Corridor to see \nprecipitation will be 2/3 PM and into the late afternoon. While \ncoverage in showers/thunderstorms are expected to be greater today\ndue to better moisture return, that does not mean that all areas \nwill see beneficial rainfall. Storms will shift into the far \neastern plains by early evening and continue through 10/11 PMish. \nSome guidance does favor additional scattered showers for the high\nterrain overnight Thursday due to an upper level shortwave. \nHowever, coverage will be limited. \n\nModels continue to favor a drying trend towards the end of the week, \nwith warmer temperatures returning by the weekend. Scattered showers \nand thunderstorms are still possible Thursday and Friday, however \ncoverage will be limited. The upper level ridge in the southwestern \nUnited States will begin to restrengthen, with 700mb temperatures \nreaching up to 20-22 dg C by Sunday. Ensembles favor portions of the \nplains reaching 100 F Sunday and possibly Monday.",
